WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.899
Film Courage: 12 questions every screenwriter
has to be able to answer about their screenplay?

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:05.460
Mark Heidelberger, Producer: 12 questions?

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.960
Why does it have to be 12 questions?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.219
How did you get the 12 questions?

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:14.650
I mentioned earlier that I was a manager,
a literary manager.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:20.750
So I did writers and directors for about 8
years.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.820
So I had…I’m going to give Meg LeFauve
who I mentioned earlier (Jodie Foster’s

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.190
producing partner) was a great influence on
me and she had this series of questions (might

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:35.980
have been eight or ten) to get us thinking
about the story we wanted to tell.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.940
When I started managing, I sort of took those
questions and expanded on them a little bit

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.800
(re-appropriated them a little bit) so I came
up with 12.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.320
And so every time I had a writer come to me
with a story they were working on….you know,

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.530
it could have been in the synopsis phase or
the treatment phase or the finished script

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.600
but had a lot of problems…okay, let’s
rewind and let’s go back to the fundamentals

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.809
or even if they just had an idea and they
hadn’t written anything yet.

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:06.510
Take a look at these questions, can you answer
these questions?

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.190
The questions are things like:

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.510
What’s the genre?

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.350
Is it studio or independent?

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:15.350
Who is your protagonist?

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.350
What is their goal?

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.360
What is their flaw?

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.460
Who is the main relationship?

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.450
What is driving the story?

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.299
Who is your audience?

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:27.299
Who is the antagonist?

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:28.299
What is their goal?

00:01:28.299 --> 00:01:29.299
What is their flaw?

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:32.780
What is the narrative question driving the
film?

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.800
So you should be able to sum it up into one
big question.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:47.560
Like DIE HARD: Will the New York cop free
his wife and hostages from some European terrorists

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.660
who are all trapped in a skyscraper?

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.680
Like it’s one question.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.560
What is your narrative question driving this
story?

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.330
What is your theme?

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.479
What is your thematic question?

00:01:56.479 --> 00:01:57.479
Why are you telling this?

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.439
A thematic question is like the question you
pose to the audience.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:02.210
And the moral question, or something else.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.930
But it’s something you want them to think
about.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.410
Like ‘Is family really people you are related
to or is it something more?’

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:09.880
It could be your thematic question.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.800
It’s not overtly stated in the story, but
it’s kind of in the film’s subconscious

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.290
(it’s what the film is kind of about).

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.400
And then your theme is how you answer that
question by the end.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.510
Those are off the top of my head.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.910
And if you can’t answer those questions
or if those questions don’t make sense together,

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.550
a lot of times there’s the problems with
the story.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.090
So for example is you say my protagonist’s
goal is…my protagonist’s goal is to grab

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:41.750
the banana.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.180
And what’s your antagonist’s goal?

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.190
To grab the orange!

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.880
You are in two different movies.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.780
They are never going to intersect, there is
no conflict.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.819
Great your protagonist grabs the banana and
your antagonist grabs the orange, movie’s

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:52.819
over.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:55.070
And that’s a very simplistic example but
those are the kinds of things on a deeper

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.770
level to look at and say there are a few things
that aren’t making sense.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.740
You say your main relationship is character
A and character B, but your antagonist is

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.000
character C. They are not even in your main
relationship that is moving the story forward,

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.210
so why are they your antagonist?

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:14.190
So the 12 questions…and this is from amateur
writers who have never had anything sold called

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.739
development cases people who you have a belief
in because they are talented and you’re

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:19.110
trying to get them there.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:25.500
All the way up to experienced screenwriters
who have had a half dozen pictures produced,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.330
the 12 questions is a great sort of reset,
okay let me square away even if I’ve won

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:36.980
this a hundred times, every story is different
and lots of times you miss those key important

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:38.040
details.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.820
And once I think those 12 questions are making
sense now, they are tying together…oh now

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.690
see how the protagonist’s goal ties into
your theme?

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:49.060
Okay…now the puzzle pieces start making
sense.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:54.610
Then I saw okay now let’s work out a synopsis,
3 to 4 paragraphs, first act, second act,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:04:00.459
third act whatever just so we can say from
a broad story standpoint those questions are

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:05.210
being addressed and there you can expand it
to a full treatment and scene outline, then

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:06.210
start writing your script.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:11.280
Like I said, it’s a lot easier to develop
a story in a synopsis form or a treatment

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.120
form then it is to keep rewriting a script
when there are fundamental problems in it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.109
You don’t start building the building and
“Oh, it’s slanted.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:19.469
Okay, let’s start taking out some bricks.”

00:04:19.469 --> 00:04:22.979
You work all of that out of the design phase
and the blueprint phase, so that’s what

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:25.159
I try to get them to do.

00:04:25.159 --> 00:04:29.629
And the 12 questions is…that’s the beginning,
the organic seed that we need to plant to

00:04:29.629 --> 00:04:31.139
make a healthy tree grow.

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:35.550
Film Courage: And when presented with those
10 questions (or whatever it is), do most

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:40.439
screenwriters see the vague areas of their
story?

00:04:40.439 --> 00:04:46.219
Or are a lot of them very sort of stuck on
those ideas and it makes sense to them but

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.330
they’re not willing to waiver?

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.240
Mark Heidelberger: No, I find that they are
usually very open.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.719
I mean, if they are not going to be open to
the process (to the creative development process),

00:04:56.719 --> 00:04:59.389
I’m not going to be able to do much with
them.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:02.719
I think most of them, they are coming to me
because they know there is something wrong,

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:03.719
you know?

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:05.330
If they think it’s great, than hey whatever.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.879
If I read the script and go “Yeah…okay?

00:05:06.879 --> 00:05:09.539
This is decent, I’ll go with it.”

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:14.090
And we might have them or bring another writer
in to do polishes or something.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:20.520
But most of the time when they come to me
to develop the script they know something

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.930
is wrong so that’s why I said let’s go
back to those fundamentals and I don’t necessarily

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.129
answer the questions for them.

00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:32.430
Sometimes I can, I’ll come up with ideas
and say well maybe when I was reading your

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.520
story I felt like your theme was a little
more about this and they’ll go “Yeah!

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:36.520
Yeah!”

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.199
Sometimes I can articulate or pull out what
they are trying to get to quicker than they

00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:41.199
can.

00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:47.219
But a lot of times it’s just me pointing
out discrepancies like “You say you want

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:52.449
to do sort of this, this and this, which makes
me feel that you want it very small and this

00:05:52.449 --> 00:05:53.449
and that.

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:54.610
But you also say you want to do a studio picture?

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.900
But is that what studios are buying?

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:56.930
Is that what studios are putting out?

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:01.659
Now I might just call out some discrepancies
and they are like “Oh, yeah okay.

00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:02.659
Maybe this is more indie?”

00:06:02.659 --> 00:06:05.300
And we discuss it, we talk about it.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.939
But I think getting on the same page, getting
those things so they are making sense makes

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:12.990
all the rest of the stuff much easier.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.400
So I don’t get a lot of push back.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.759
I don’t get a lot of pushback because a
lot of it’s coming from them, it’s just

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:18.369
me pointing it out, guiding them.

